 "limits": [
  "reachability is intra-procedural and syntactic: a loop that reassigns the name inside its body, or a binding reached through a helper, is not modelled",
  "the iteration class enumerates only a fully literal iterable; anything else answers unresolved, and unresolved is published as an instrument limit, never as a zero",
  "the iteration class is a per-path statement: a row proven there is NOT proven unconditionally, and the two counts are published separately for that reason",
  "no runner and no fixture write: this study reads source and E635's published rows"
 ],
 "verdict": "the_after_side_stays_unresolved_and_at_least_one_of_those_locations_carries_no_read_of_the_counted_key_below_the_receiver",
 "answer": "Of E635's 12 row(s), 1 change class once binding reachability replaces the union of every same-named binding in the scope.  Along the iteration of the enclosing literal loop that binds the counted key: 1 are proven to be that key, 0 could be, 5 are another key and 6 stay unresolved.  On the after side of the dead line that is 0 proven / 0 could-be / 1 other / 5 unresolved, over 7 enumerated iteration(s) of which 1 carry the key.  4 of the 5 after-side unresolved location(s) do read the counted key -- one level below the receiver (cutoff, eq_s, k, key, x1, x2), where E633's classifier folds the read out of the census instead of counting it."
}
